## Hard requirements (violating any of these makes the output unusable)
1. `#!/usr/bin/python3` shebang; `import sys`; `from framework import ATTestSet, ATTestCase` (plus any other framework modules you actually use — ATDrivers, ATLibrary helpers, ATPackets are all allowed and encouraged where they fit).
2. One `class TestSet(ATTestSet.TestSet)` with `FEATURES = ['ALL']`, an `init(self, setup)` declaring the topology via `setup.init_tb()/init_stk()/init_swi()/init_portlink()`, a `configure(self)` for one-time CLI config, and a `tear_down(self)` that restores the device state.
3. One `class TestCase_<n>(ATTestCase.TestCase)` per sequence entry, in order, each with:
   - `testCaseDesc` = one-line description derived from the entry's action
   - `testCaseRef  = '{{ case_key }}'`
   - `testCaseMethod` = the entry's action + verify text (may use += accumulation)
   - `main(self)` that performs the action (CLI via `dut.mode(...)` / `dut.cmd(...)`, traffic via Scapy/tcpdump on the testbox) and asserts with `self.passed('reason')` / `self.failed('reason')`. Every main() must call at least one of them.
4. Reach devices from a TestCase via `self.testSet.<name>` (e.g. `self.testSet.dutA`).
5. `if __name__ == '__main__':` block that builds `ts = TestSet()`, calls `ts.add_testCase(TestCase_<n>())` for every case in order, then `ts.run(sys.argv)`.
6. Adapt the approved fragments rather than writing everything fresh; keep their proven CLI commands and parsing. Match their code style (naming, logging with self.log()).
7. No placeholder/pseudo code, no TODOs, no external pip dependencies beyond what the fragments/exemplar already use.

